            <ArticleTitle>Vanishing pineal mass in a young patient without therapy: Case report and review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>303-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/1971400916655480</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Spontaneous regression is defined as the decrease in size or disappearance of a primary tumour or metastatic disease without therapeutic intervention. Computed tomography and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the brain was performed in a 14-year-old girl experiencing sudden-onset headaches. First diagnostic exams revealed a mass in the pineal region that disappeared at three-month MRI control. Three-year follow-up MRI studies were performed. No therapy was administered, but the pineal lesion had disappeared at three-year follow-up.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© The Author(s) 2016.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
